        "text": "ADVANCED 7/9: DATA-DRIVEN LOGIC (arrays, maps, structs)\nThe catalogue lives in a String variable (`item_table`: \"name:price,name:price\") that designers edit in the\nlevel, not in the graph. Logic reads the table at run time: Split to an array, walk it with a loop,\nsplit each row again. Arrays and maps are VALUES that flow on wires (variables hold bool, integers,\nfloats, String, Entity and object types such as Vec3), so build collections inside one chain.\nStruct-like values (Vec3, Vec2, Transform) have Make/Break style nodes.",
        "position": [
          0,
          -330
        ],
        "size": [
          1500,
          230
        ],
        "color": [
          0.9,
          0.75,
          0.2,
          0.28
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-2",
        "text": "1. ARRAYS - `Split` turns \"30,10,20\" into an array of Strings. Array Push appends \"5\",\nArray Join turns it back into text, Array Contains queries it (Array Length feeds the loop in step 3). Array nodes RETURN the\nchanged array on `result` rather than mutating: wire the output into the next node. Their element type is\ninferred from a typed wire, so start from a typed source such as Split (an `Array New` with nothing connected\ncannot know its element type). Note: Array Get/First/Last/Pop give TWO outputs (`present`, `value`); this\nexample reads elements with Array Slice + Array Join instead (see step 3).",
        "position": [
          -30,
          -70
        ],
        "size": [
          3040,
          710
        ],
        "color": [
          0.25,
          0.45,
          0.75,
          0.22
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "bp",
          "a_src",
          "a_p1",
          "a_join",
          "a_has",
          "a_has_s",
          "a_cat1",
          "a_cat2",
          "a_log"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-3",
        "text": "2. MAPS - `HashMap New`, then `HashMap Insert` twice (the pure nodes return the NEW map). The\nmap keys and values are Strings. They are pure, so they run when the JSON text is needed. Use\n`Contains Key`, `Keys` and `Values` to query one. Maps cannot be stored in variables; keep them in\none chain or rebuild them from the data table.",
        "position": [
          -30,
          620
        ],
        "size": [
          2020,
          480
        ],
        "color": [
          0.25,
          0.65,
          0.35,
          0.22
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "bm",
          "m_new",
          "m_i1",
          "m_i2",
          "m_json",
          "m_cat",
          "m_log"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-4",
        "text": "3. DATA-DRIVEN LOOKUP - `buy(item)` walks the table. Split the table at commas into rows; For Loop\nonce per row (count = Array Length). The loop has no index pin, so `row_index` is our own counter:\nreset to 0 first, incremented by the loop body's second Sequence output (Then1) on EVERY iteration.\nThen0 does the work for row i: read it with Array Slice(i, i+1) joined back to text; it matches when it\nstarts with \"<item>:\". Split the row at the colon, slice element 1 (the price), parse it to an integer\nand compare with `gold`. Changing the catalogue needs no graph edit. (Loops always run to the end; a\nmatch simply does its work once because each row is visited once.)",
        "position": [
          -30,
          1540
